The Schwarzschild radius (event horizon radius) of a non-rotating black hole is given by:

\( r_s = \frac{2GM}{c^2} \),

where \( G \) is the gravitational constant (\(6.674 \times 10^{-11}\,\text{m}^3\text{kg}^{-1}\text{s}^{-2}\)), \( M \) is the mass of the object, and \( c \) is the speed of light in vacuum (\(2.998 \times 10^8\,\text{m/s}\)).

We are given that the mass is at least three solar masses. One solar mass (\(M_{\odot}\)) is approximately \(1.989 \times 10^{30}\,\text{kg}\). Therefore:

\( M = 3 M_{\odot} = 3 \times 1.989 \times 10^{30}\,\text{kg}
   = 5.967 \times 10^{30}\,\text{kg}. \)

Now compute the Schwarzschild radius:

\( r_s = \frac{2 (6.674 \times 10^{-11}\,\text{m}^3\text{kg}^{-1}\text{s}^{-2}) (5.967 \times 10^{30}\,\text{kg})}{(2.998 \times 10^8\,\text{m/s})^2}. \)

First calculate the numerator:

\( 2 \times 6.674 \times 10^{-11} \times 5.967 \times 10^{30}
   = 13.348 \times 5.967 \times 10^{19}
   \approx 79.639 \times 10^{19}
   = 7.9639 \times 10^{20}\,\text{m}^3\text{s}^{-2}. \)

Next calculate the denominator:

\( (2.998 \times 10^8)^2 = 8.988 \times 10^{16}\,\text{m}^2\text{s}^{-2}. \)

Now divide:

\( r_s = \frac{7.9639 \times 10^{20}}{8.988 \times 10^{16}}
       \approx \frac{7.9639}{8.988} \times 10^{4}
       \approx 0.8865 \times 10^{4}
       = 8.865 \times 10^{3}\,\text{m}. \)

Thus the radius is approximately \(8.9 \times 10^3\) meters. This corresponds to the coefficient X ≈ 8.9.

